  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["PR1", "H1"],
      "pins": ["PR1.VCC", "PR1.SDA", "PR1.SCL", "PR1.GND", "H1.1", "H1.2", "H1.3", "H1.4"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "I2C_SDA", "I2C_SCL", "GND"],
      "observed": "In the 'WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES' block the TF-Luna module PR1 (LIDAR) is drawn wired through header H1 (PB_1.25mm): VCC to +5V, SDA to the I2C_SDA label, SCL to the I2C_SCL label, and GND to ground. PR1 is not crossed out / DNP (unlike PR5/PR6).",
      "expected": "The specification states the LiDAR is intentionally unwired in this revision: 'the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no ... and all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net', repeated in Known Limitations ('TF-Luna LiDAR is unwired ... not connected to any net').",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "PR1 LIDAR ... H1 PB_1.25mm ... +5V / I2C_SDA / I2C_SCL / GND (sheet 1, 'Water Level Sensor/Switches')"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free — i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design"}
      ],
      "impact": "A device the reference design leaves floating is placed on the shared, non-isolated I2C bus and on the +5V rail. The TF-Luna's I2C address can load or hold the bus and is never initialised by the firmware (which has no uart/distance block for it), so bus behaviour and 5V loading differ from the intended board.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["T5", "T3"],
      "pins": ["T5.G", "T5.D", "T4.C", "T3.D", "T3.S", "R7"],
      "nets": ["ATO_VALVE", "/+24V", "/Gate_ATO"],
      "observed": "The 'ATO Valve' driver uses T4 (BT3904) -> R10 -> gate of T5, which is an AO3400A (small-signal SOT-23 N-MOS) switching the ATO_VALVE load. Meanwhile T3, an AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOS), is placed as a series element in the +24V input path (labelled 'Reverse polarity protection', gate pulled to GND via R7 33k).",
      "expected": "Spec §2 assigns the AON6407 (T3) as the ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch: 'T3 — AON6407 ... ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch ... gate driven through R10 (100 Ω), pre-driven via T4 (BT3904)'. Spec §3's 24V power tree contains no series MOSFET (F1 -> D2 -> +24V).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ATO Valve: ATO_VALVE -> R10 100R -> T4 BT3904 -> T5 AO3400A; Power Train: F1 -> T3 AON6407 'Reverse polarity protection' with R7 33k to GND (sheet 1)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch | ... gate driven through R10 (100 Ω), pre-driven via T4 (BT3904)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The valve is switched by a small-signal FET instead of the intended power FET (reduced current/thermal margin), and the 24V rail now passes through an N-MOS used as a high-side reverse-polarity element that the intended architecture does not include (gate-to-GND drive cannot enhance an N-MOS high-side, risking a diode-drop / non-conduction on the main rail).",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build deviates from the intended design in two places: the TF-Luna LiDAR (PR1), which the spec documents as unwired/free, is in this schematic connected to the +5V rail and the main I2C bus via H1; and the ATO-valve switch is implemented with T5 (AO3400A) driven by R10/T4 instead of the intended T3 (AON6407), with T3 instead inserted as a series element in the 24V input that the spec's power tree omits."
}

